":\\\"Research\\\",\\\"item\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research\\\"}]},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"FAQPage\\\",\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research#faq\\\",\\\"mainEntity\\\":[{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"What is TheTaxCalc Research?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc Research is our collection of original data studies and reference guides on U.S. taxation. Each study combines official government data — IRS, SSA, Census Bureau, and state revenue departments — with our own analysis to produce practical, accurate, and fully-sourced tax insights. Every study is reviewed by a credentialed tax professional before publication.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"How often is the research updated?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"We update each study on its natural refresh cycle. State tax burden and property tax data refresh annually after new Census and Tax Foundation releases. Child Tax Credit and OBBBA-related research updates whenever Congress or the IRS issues new guidance. Tax refund statistics update quarterly during the filing season (January through April) and once after the season ends.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Can I cite TheTaxCalc research in my own work?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Yes. We encourage journalists, researchers, financial advisors, and educators to cite our research. Each study includes a permanent URL, publication date, and last-updated date so your readers can verify the source. Please link to the original study rather than copying the data tables wholesale. If you need a higher-resolution chart or raw data export, contact us at hello@thetaxcalc.com.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Where does your data come from?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Every figure in our research comes from an official government source. Federal tax data is sourced from IRS publications (Rev. Proc. 2025-25, Pub. 15-T, Pub. 972) and the Social Security Administration. State tax data comes from each state's Department of Revenue. Demographic and income data comes from the U.S. Census Bureau. Cost of living data comes from the Bureau of Economic Analysis and the Council for Community and Economic Research. We publish the full methodology and source list at the bottom of every study.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Is TheTaxCalc research free to access?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Yes. Every study, data table, and chart in our research hub is free to read, with no paywall, no sign-up, and no email requirement. We do not collect your contact information when you read our research. We believe tax data should be freely accessible to every American. If you find the research useful, we encourage you to share it with colleagues or link to it from your own writing.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"How is this research different from your blog?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Our blog covers timely tax news, how-to guides, and current-year updates. Our research hub, by contrast, contains original data-driven studies with a longer shelf life. Research pieces are typically longer (7-12 minutes of reading), include multiple data tables and charts, and undergo a more rigorous editorial review process. Both are written by credentialed tax professionals and sourced from official government data.\\\"}}]},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"CollectionPage\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc Research Hub\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Original tax research and data studies from TheTaxCalc, covering state tax burden, property tax, the Child Tax Credit, remote work, and tax refund statistics.\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research\\\",\\\"inLanguage\\\":\\\"en-US\\\",\\\"isPartOf\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"WebSite\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com\\\"},\\\"hasPart\\\":[{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ScholarlyArticle\\\",\\\"headline\\\":\\\"2026 State Tax Burden: Total Tax Load by State\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research/2026-state-tax-burden\\\",\\\"datePublished\\\":\\\"2026-01-15\\\",\\\"author\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"},\\\"publisher\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ScholarlyArticle\\\",\\\"headline\\\":\\\"Best States for Remote Workers in 2026\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research/best-states-for-remote-workers-2026\\\",\\\"datePublished\\\":\\\"2026-01-15\\\",\\\"author\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"},\\\"publisher\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ScholarlyArticle\\\",\\\"headline\\\":\\\"Child Tax Credit Guide 2026 — OBBBA Doubles CTC to $2,200\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research/child-tax-credit-guide-2026\\\",\\\"datePublished\\\":\\\"2026-01-15\\\",\\\"author\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"},\\\"publisher\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ScholarlyArticle\\\",\\\"headline\\\":\\\"Property Tax by State 2026 — Effective Rates \\u0026 Median Bills\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research/property-tax-by-state-2026\\\",\\\"datePublished\\\":\\\"2026-01-15\\\",\\\"author\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"},\\\"publisher\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ScholarlyArticle\\\",\\\"headline\\\":\\\"Salary Needed to Live Comfortably in 2026 — By State\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/research/salary-needed-to-live-comfortably-2026\\\"